{"reference":"1 John 5","book":"1 John","chapter":5,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"chapter_overview","title":"Overview","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"The chapter joins belief in Jesus as the Son of God with love, obedience, and victory over “the world.” Here “conquering” is a metaphor for confession and faithfulness, not permission for religious coercion, nationalism, or violence. Its later promise about prayer is also qualified: confidence rests in asking according to God’s will, rather than in a guarantee of wealth, healing, or any particular outcome.","verse_refs":["1 John 5:1-5","1 John 5:14-15"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_1_john_005_9716c322e814eb50e3b829ec"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"inference"},{"id":"block_2","text":"Verses 9–13 concentrate the chapter’s testimony on the Son: God’s witness concerns the Son, and eternal life is said to be in the Son. The repeated language of witness gives these claims the weight of attestation, though it should not be read as a transcript of an ordinary court case.","verse_refs":["1 John 5:9-13"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_1_john_005_7404da56059f0bcfe07fc082"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"interpretive_questions","title":"Interpretive Questions","blocks":[{"id":"block_3","text":"“Water and blood” in verses 6–8 has been understood in several ways, including references to baptism, death, incarnation, sacramental practice, or a response to opponents; the passage itself does not settle the question. The Spirit’s testimony belongs to the same argument. Some later forms of the text add a longer statement about heavenly witnesses in verses 7–8, but that wording is secondary to the earliest recoverable Greek text.","verse_refs":["1 John 5:6-12","1 John 5:7-8"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_1_john_005_55e4a80360d7a67ed7423158","reader_path_1_john_005_1f79e76bf00becedf41c72a5"],"confidence":"low","interpretation_level":"disputed"},{"id":"block_4","text":"The “sin unto death” in verses 16–17 is left undefined. The writer distinguishes it from sin that is not unto death and qualifies the instruction about prayer, so the passage does not authorize readers to declare particular people beyond prayer or hope.","verse_refs":["1 John 5:16-17"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_1_john_005_55e4a80360d7a67ed7423158"],"confidence":"low","interpretation_level":"disputed"},{"id":"block_5","text":"Verse 18 speaks of those born of God being guarded and untouched by the evil one. This is theological assurance, not a promise that believers will never experience suffering or harm, and it should not be used to blame those who do.","verse_refs":["1 John 5:18"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_1_john_005_55e4a80360d7a67ed7423158"],"confidence":"low","interpretation_level":"disputed"},{"id":"block_6","text":"The final sentences leave two notable questions open. In verse 20, “the true God and eternal life” may grammatically refer to Jesus Christ or to the Father. Verse 21 then ends abruptly with a warning against idols; the warning may be literal, metaphorical, or a summary of the letter’s concerns. Neither uncertainty supports treating the passage as authorization to destroy others’ property, cultures, or sacred sites.","verse_refs":["1 John 5:20","1 John 5:21"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_1_john_005_1f79e76bf00becedf41c72a5"],"confidence":"low","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"surrounding_passages","title":"The Work as a Whole","blocks":[{"id":"block_7","text":"The work does not name its writer, its addressees, their location, or its destination. Readers should therefore avoid treating those details as settled by the text itself.","verse_refs":["1 John 1:1-10","1 John 2:1-29","1 John 3:1-24","1 John 4:1-21"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_1_john_005_8f4675493d5185d09ab05557"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"fact"}]}],"generated_metadata":null}